From 823e768ac9ef7233a6ba9a060582ee43c3221035 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ruscher <rruscher@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 02:26:15 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 22/23] dock: a docked application's command launches it; a
 click brings back a tray app

When the icon of a running application is docked, Window Maker kept its
command line (WM_COMMAND, from /proc/<pid>/cmdline) as the icon's command.
That often launches nothing: a script that renamed itself ("biglinux-tts
main.py"), a program or a file relative to the working directory. The
command is now gtk-launch of the application's .desktop file (StartupWMClass,
the desktop id or the program of Exec matching WM_CLASS or the executable;
an id ending in ".desktop" keeps the suffix, as gtk-launch needs), else the
command line with the program and the relative files made absolute, quoted
for ExpandOptions and wtokensplit. Dockapps keep theirs.

A double-click on the docked icon of an application that runs without any
window (Telegram or Steam closed to the tray) only unhid nothing: it now
runs the command, which brings the window back for single-instance
applications.

make check: test/appcommand_test.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
